        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> So rust-analyzer publishes diagnostics for the whole workspace and I tried to make it work with this snippet, but I'm not that proficient in lua and there is no icons, can I have some guidance on how to do it properly for mini.files?\n\nTwo quick things that caught attention in this snippet:\n- There is no icons because default `prefix` is responsible for that. To have both icons and diagnostics as prefix, you'd have to explicitly incorporate icons there also. Maybe following something like [this](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/48f48e4b3f317e9da34ee7a01958b4c5018e2d34/doc/mini-files.txt#L426-L432).\n- Calling `get_max_severity_for_files()` inside `prefix` is very not efficient, as it will be called for *every file* in the directory (while computing diagnostics for all files each time). Doing that efficiently is [the concern in this comment](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1797#issuecomment-2881200225).\n\nMy suggestion would be to manually show this type of icons via extmarks and not prefix. And do that inside dedicated events for efficiency. Maybe something like this:\n- Compute and cache project diagnostic only [when explorer is opened](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/48f48e4b3f317e9da34ee7a01958b4c5018e2d34/doc/mini-files.txt#L328).\n- (Re)Add extmarks [when path's buffer is updated](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/48f48e4b3f317e9da34ee7a01958b4c5018e2d34/doc/mini-files.txt#L336).\n\nYou can take a look at [this gist](https://gist.github.com/bassamsdata/eec0a3065152226581f8d4244cce9051#file-notes-md) that adds Git signs for inspiration (side note: I didn't read the full source code, so specifics might be suboptimal).",
